First, we begin with the first inequality.  This means that the distance from any point $(x,y)$ on the region to the point $(4,0)$ is at most $4$.  Hence, the region is contained within a circle of radius $4$ centered at $(4,0)$.  The second inequality means that the region is above the line $y=x-4$.  The third inequality means that the region is above the line $y=-\frac{1}{3}x$.  See below for a graph of all three inequalities.  The intersection of the three is the region whose area we need to compute.  Using symmetry, it is clear that the region is $\frac{1}{4}$ of a circle of radius $4$ with two right triangles removed.  The areas of the Circle, triangle above $y=-\frac{1}{3}x$, and the triangle below $y=x-4$ are all easily computed as $16\pi$, $8$, and $4$, respectively.  Hence, the area of the region is $\frac{1}{4}(16\pi)-8-4=4\pi-12$.  \footnotesize \begin{align*}
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[thick] (0,0)--(8,0);
\draw[thick] (-1,0)--(8,0);
\draw[thick] (0,-4)--(0,4);
\draw[thick] (4,0)--(4,4);
\draw[thick,dashed] (-2,6)--(6,2);
\draw[thick] (-2,6)--(4,0);
\draw[thick,red] (4,0)--(6,2);
\fill[fill=gray!30] (0,0) ellipse (4 and 4);
\fill[fill=black] (8,0)--(8,-1.5)--(6,2)--cycle;
\fill[fill=black] (-2,6)--(-2,0)--(0,0)--cycle;
\draw[fill=black] (4,0) circle (2pt);
\draw[fill=black] (6,2) circle (2pt);
\draw[fill=black] (8,0) circle (2pt);
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{align*} \large </think> <answer> $4\pi-12$ </answer>

